WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.029 --> 00:00:02.850
(upbeat funky music)

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:03.990
- When this plane flies,

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:06.360
the only emission that
comes out of its tailpipe

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.360
is the same thing that's
in this glass, water.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.820
It's because this plane is
powered by a hydrogen fuel cell.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.650
Now I'll go into how
this works in a second,

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.290
but what's important is that
some in the aviation industry

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.760
believe that this technology,

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:21.930
hydrogen as a fuel,

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.990
could hold the key to
decarbonizing flight.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.430
- What we need to do is eliminate

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.410
the climate impact of flying.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:31.470
Hydrogen looks like it
can allow us to do that.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.990
- But the physics and
economics around hydrogen

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.610
mean that the odds of your future flight

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.040
being hydrogen-powered is very dependent

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.870
on how far you plan on flying.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:41.670
So is hydrogen flight here,

00:00:41.670 --> 00:00:44.133
is it in the future, or
is it already too late?

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.270
Before we get into how
planes like this work,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.850
it's important to consider
why the aviation industry

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:52.320
is thinking about hydrogen at all.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:55.260
In 2021, aviation was responsible

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.010
for over 2% of global
energy-related CO2 emissions.

00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:02.160
With flights pumping out
a little over 720 megatons

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.690
of CO2 into the atmosphere,

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.190
and before the pandemic

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.640
that was even higher,
at over 1,000 megatons.

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.980
Even though engines are
getting more fuel efficient

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:13.290
as global travel continues to recover,

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.540
aviation emissions are growing.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.280
So in order to hit targets laid out

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:18.840
in the Paris Climate Accords,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:21.240
various aviation groups such as IATA,

00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.400
the International Air
Transport Association,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:25.890
have set the industry an ambitious target,

00:01:25.890 --> 00:01:28.800
net zero emissions by 2050.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.030
While alternative fuels

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.580
like sustainable aviation fuel, or SAF,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.490
could provide a pathway to net zero,

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.320
as the fuel still emits CO2,

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.320
hydrogen could provide a route
to absolutely zero emissions.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:43.920
So how's it work?

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:46.200
One method is to use hydrogen combustion

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.060
where the hydrogen replaces jet fuel

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.550
and is ignited to drive the propellers.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.980
Now, hydrogen is highly flammable.

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:55.980
So while that means you get
a lot of bang for your buck,

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:58.770
almost three times that
of jet fuel by weight,

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.290
it also means that its
use in aviation history

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:02.793
has been somewhat checkered.

00:02:03.707 --> 00:02:05.460
(Hindenburg exploding)

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.410
So the other less fiery way

00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.480
to use hydrogen in planes like this

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:11.520
is with a hydrogen fuel cell.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:12.390
Much like a battery,

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:13.770
hydrogen fuel cells work

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.680
by creating a flow of
electrons around a circuit.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:19.440
Hydrogen is fed into one
side of a cell, the anode,

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.570
and oxygen into the other, the cathode,

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.880
with a membrane sat between the two.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.460
At the anode, the hydrogen
flows over a catalyst,

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.160
which separates the protons and electrons.

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.540
Now, the hydrogen protons

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.660
are able to pass through the
membrane to reach the oxygen,

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.670
but the electrons can't.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.190
Instead, they have to make
their way around a circuit

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:40.170
and by doing so, power a motor

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.510
until they can reunite
with the hydrogen protons

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:46.350
and the oxygen to form H2O or water,

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:47.610
which along with heat

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.790
is the only emission that
comes from this whole process.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.100
And it's that exact principle

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.870
that's happening in this plane behind me.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.133
This is ZeroAvia's test plane,

00:02:57.133 --> 00:02:58.950
a 19-seat aircraft,

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.050
which had its debut
hydrogen-powered flight

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.460
earlier this year.

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:05.580
I caught up with ZeroAvia's
CEO at the Paris Air Show.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.160
- Today, we have four out
of the top 10 airlines

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:09.510
as our customers,
- Oh. right.

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.670
- already people reserve production slots

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:13.080
because they want to be the first.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:15.600
- Right now, it's only
this left-hand engine

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.890
that's hydrogen-powered,

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:18.930
but once the company completes testing

00:03:18.930 --> 00:03:20.820
it has plans to develop engines

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:22.830
to be able to carry 19 passengers

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.893
around 300 nautical miles by 2025.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.370
Two other small startups
pursuing hydrogen flight,

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:31.560
are US-based Universal Hydrogen

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.960
who were flown a similar
plane to ZeroAvia,

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:35.940
and German-based H2FLY.

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:37.740
- We started in 2006

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.840
to develop this kind of technology.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:40.673
At the moment,

00:03:40.673 --> 00:03:43.890
we are in the seventh
generation of powertrains,

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:47.040
and I think the technology is today here,

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.320
- [Narrator] But it's
not just small startups

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:50.940
or small aircraft,

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:53.040
one major plane manufacturer

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:57.300
has thrown its weight behind
hydrogen in a big way, Airbus.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:58.800
- We're hoping to introduce

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:01.668
a hydrogen-powered aircraft in 2035,

00:04:01.668 --> 00:04:02.850
with zero CO2,

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:04.590
so we're not just talking about net zero,

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:06.660
we're talking about absolute zero.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:07.493
- Airbus has plans

00:04:07.493 --> 00:04:09.990
for three different
commercial hydrogen planes,

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:12.540
a turbofan, a propeller-powered plane,

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.240
and a longer range
further into the future,

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:16.860
blended wing design.

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:20.310
- Our view is that 2035
is not soon enough,

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:21.660
and we've got an urgency

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.970
to be bringing these
technologies to the market.

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.160
- But while Airbus may
be backing hydrogen,

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:27.330
Boeing on the other hand,

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.750
appears less certain about
its role in powering aircraft.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.190
- We've learned a lot with hydrogen,

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:33.023
and we've flown

00:04:33.023 --> 00:04:35.480
six different forms of
airplanes on hydrogen.

00:04:35.480 --> 00:04:39.180
So we just think that its
impact on airplanes, airports,

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:40.710
and pipelines is greater,

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:43.350
and therefore, it won't help us as soon

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.670
as some of these other solutions

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.050
that are less impactful.
- [Narrator] Yeah.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:47.430
- [Chris] And if the goal is 2050,

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.980
we've gotta work on the things
that'll help us the fastest.

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.210
- So why is that?

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:52.230
Well, while hydrogen

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:55.230
might be one of the most abundant
elements in the Universe,

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.540
on Earth, it's almost always attached

00:04:57.540 --> 00:05:00.420
to something else, like
oxygen to form water.

00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.000
To isolate it requires
using an electrolyzer,

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:04.950
which is the reverse of a fuel cell,

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.450
and that requires energy.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:07.800
Now, in order for hydrogen fuel

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.330
to be considered carbon neutral,

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:10.890
known as green hydrogen,

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:13.740
the energy source that you
use in that electrolyzer,

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.380
also has to be renewable, like solar.

00:05:16.380 --> 00:05:19.200
And right now, green hydrogen is expensive

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.850
and kind of hard to come by.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:22.680
- The different decarbonized energies

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:23.513
that we're looking at

00:05:23.513 --> 00:05:26.400
are more expensive than kerosene is today.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.070
So there's going to be
an increase in price,

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:30.420
in cost of the airlines,

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:33.480
and that's likely to have
an impact on ticket prices.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:36.150
- Then there's the infrastructure
around delivering it.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:38.190
While hydrogen electrolyzers can be set up

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:39.360
pretty much anywhere,

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:42.090
the amount needed to power
aviation around the globe

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:43.410
would require investment,

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:45.990
potentially more than
other sustainable fuels

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:47.640
that can be piped into airports

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.980
using the same infrastructure as jet fuel.

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:52.080
- We expect that in Europe,

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.490
we will have 10 to 12
billion of Euros investment

00:05:56.490 --> 00:06:00.630
just to replace the carnosine
infrastructure with hydrogen.

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:02.280
- And then there's the plane itself.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.890
While hydrogen may be
lighter than jet fuel,

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.350
it's actually less energy-dense

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:10.650
and takes more space than the
equivalent amount of jet fuel.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:12.540
That's why the windows on Airbus rendered

00:06:12.540 --> 00:06:15.000
on their hydrogen aircraft stop here

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.580
because all of this would
be used as a fuel tank.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.190
Obviously, less seats
means less passengers,

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.770
which potentially means less revenue.

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.240
What's more, even with all that space,

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:26.250
right now it's still only enough hydrogen

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.720
to do short flights,

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:29.460
meaning that trans-oceanic routes

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.680
aren't currently possible on hydrogen,

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:33.480
unless it's a small ocean.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:36.360
- To really unleash the power of hydrogen,

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:38.520
to really have a huge benefit

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:41.130
even to go long range with hydrogen,

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.740
we have to rethink the way we fly.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.110
We have to rethink the shape of the planes

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:48.060
- And therein lies the problem.

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:49.440
When it comes to larger planes,

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.560
it could already be too late
for hydrogen to take off.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.210
The planes that are on sale today

00:06:54.210 --> 00:06:55.800
by the likes of Boeing and Airbus

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.250
have a backlog of roughly six
years depending on the plane.

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:00.780
Those planes are then in service

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:03.090
for an average of around 20 years.

00:07:03.090 --> 00:07:05.370
And that means that the planes
that are being sold today

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:08.250
are the planes that
we'll be using in 2050,

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:09.630
and looking around,

00:07:09.630 --> 00:07:12.030
none of them are hydrogen compatible.

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:15.390
- So hydrogen in its raw
form isn't compatible

00:07:15.390 --> 00:07:17.070
with those existing aircraft.

00:07:17.070 --> 00:07:18.840
And that's why sustainable aviation fuel

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:19.980
is really important.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:22.620
- There's also a chance
that it could stay that way.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.350
See, that target set by IATA and others

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:29.430
was for net zero by
2050, not absolute zero.

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:33.210
And if airlines achieve their
net zero aims using SAF,

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.230
there's questions around

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.510
whether they would then
choose to invest millions

00:07:36.510 --> 00:07:40.200
into renewing their entire
fleet to be hydrogen planes,

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.070
and they've technically,

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.680
already hit their environmental targets.

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:45.360
All this means that while you may see

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.280
a greater number of hydrogen planes

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:49.080
in the skies over the next few years

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.720
unless you're taking a
relatively short trip,

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.060
you could be waiting a long time

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.310
before you step foot
aboard a hydrogen plane.

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:00.623
(upbeat music)

